<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Dueñas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1810">1810</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Dueñas was a conservative Salvadoran politician who held the presidency several times between 1851 and 1871. Backed by the Guatemalan dictator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a>, who overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a> in 1863, he ruled from 1863 to 1871. He was overthrown by a liberal revolt in 1871, after which liberals held power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> permanently.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-duenas.jpg" alt="Francisco Dueñas" width="311" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Duenas,_Francisco.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Dueñas was born on 3 December 1810 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, then part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, to José Miguel Dueñas and Secundina Díaz, a wealthy family. At 17 he entered the Santo Domingo Seminary to become a priest, but left in 1829 when the Guatemalan government had the seminary dismiss its students. He took a doctorate in law at the University of San Carlos in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in 1836, and in 1837 was elected to the Federal Congress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>.</p><p>In 1840 he resigned a post under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> in protest at the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. He sat on the Supreme Court of Justice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> and in 1843 founded the newspaper El Amigo del Pueblo, which criticised President Francisco Malespín. He served as minister under Joaquín Eufrasio Guzmán and Eugenio Aguilar, and was rector of the University of El Salvador from 1850 to 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>As first presidential designate he became acting president on 12 January 1851, while Doroteo Vasconcelos invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> and was defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-arada">Battle of La Arada</a>. Elected unopposed in January 1852, he served until 1 February 1854 and signed a peace treaty with Guatemala. As vice president under Rafael Campo he was acting president twice in 1856 and sent 700 soldiers to fight <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a>'s filibusters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a>.</p><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gerardo-barrios">Gerardo Barrios</a> took power in 1859, Dueñas went into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>. Proclaimed provisional president on 26 October 1863, he issued a new constitution in March 1864 and won that year's election virtually unopposed. When Barrios rebelled after Carrera's death in 1865 and was captured, Dueñas approved his death sentence, and Barrios was shot. Dueñas changed the flag and coat of arms, founded a military college with French help in 1867, was re-elected in 1869 after the constitution was amended, and opened the National Palace in 1870. Marshal Santiago González overthrew him on 15 April 1871, during a war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honduras">Honduras</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Expelled with his family in 1872, he lived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. He tried to return in 1878 and again in 1883, fleeing each time when President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-zaldivar">Rafael Zaldívar</a> accused him of plotting. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a> on 4 March 1884, and his remains were buried in Nueva San Salvador in 1886. He was the last conservative president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pio-romero-bosque">Pío Romero Bosque</a> in 1927.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Dueñas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The conservative <b>Francisco Dueñas</b> ruled until 1871, after which liberals held power permanently.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">Republic of El Salvador · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-francisco">San Francisco</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1858" aria-label="San Salvador on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2106 1654 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco Dueñas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2556 1924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2556 1924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1858">What was here in 1858? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
